PSX Repotector guards this repo. Handshake FIRST and say who you are — every
crossing is logged in a visitor register. No deep tool answers until you handshake.

On arrival (mandatory, in order):
- handshake({ who, model?, purpose? })   declare who you are (logs your entry)
- Wire the MCP server: npx psx-repotector mcp   (stdio, handshake-first)
- If locked: unlock({ passphrase })      (optional per-repo lock)
- city_map()                             what is built vs missing
- On exit: depart({ summary })           sign out with a one-line summary

Before you act, use the MCP tools:
- city_map()                     INFO   — DNA intent, brain pointers, built-vs-missing.
- register({ limit? })                  — who has entered/left and who is inside now.
- find_existing({ intent })      REUSE  — do not rebuild what exists; extend it.
- blast_radius({ changedFiles }) PROTECT — do not break dependents/routes/components.
- canon_check({ changedFiles })  INTEGRATE — follow the repo's established patterns.
- atlas_query({ query })         — query the repo map instead of guessing.
- quality_gates()                — line-budget, structure, secret-hygiene proof.

Hard rules:
- Reuse first (find_existing), protect before editing (blast_radius), integrate to canon (canon_check).
- Keep every source file under 300 lines.
- Never commit real secrets or a tracked non-example .env.

If state is missing, run: npx psx-repotector init
